Only rewrite the subtitle element when the active cue actually changes.

updateSubtitleText() is driven by the <video> element's `timeupdate` event,
which fires roughly four times a second. It unconditionally re-assigns
innerHTML on the subtitle element, so the subtitle DOM is rebuilt ~4x/second
even while the very same cue is on screen. Measured on 10.11.11: 35 rewrites
in 15 seconds for 2 actual cue changes.

Besides the needless layout work, this makes the subtitle element unusable
for anything that augments it -- a browser extension that wraps each word in
a span to offer per-word translation has its spans destroyed within 400ms,
continuously, for as long as the cue is displayed.

This keeps the last rendered cue per subtitle slot (primary / secondary) and
skips the DOM write when it has not changed. Cue transitions and the hide/show
behaviour are unaffected. After the patch: rewrites equal cue changes exactly.

Upstream: not yet submitted.

